+## Mods
+
+Mods are files, not settings, and come in two kinds. A **RomFS mod** only replaces or adds data, and needs no rebuild: the `rom` mount layers it over the game's RomFS at run time (`dm/host/hle_fs.c`). A **code mod** changes a module (`main`, `subsdk*`, `sdk`), which iOS cannot load from a document: the app runs only the modules embedded in its signed executable, so a code mod has to be applied before the rewrite and packed into a new IPA.
+
+- **The folder.** `DM_MODS_DIR=<dir>[:<dir>…]` lists the mods folders, in order. The iOS app sets it to `Documents/mods/<title ID>` and, when the build carries mods, `<App>/mods/<title ID>` after it; a `Documents/env.txt` or launch environment's own `DM_MODS_DIR` wins. The Mac launcher does not set it.
+- **The layout.** Each subfolder of a mods folder is one mod. Its `romfs/` is the overlay root when it has one; otherwise the mod folder itself is (files directly under it). A mods folder that is itself one mod (it has `romfs/`) is taken as one. This accepts Atmosphère's `contents/<title ID>/<mod>/romfs/…`, Ryujinx/yuzu's `mods/contents/<title ID>/…`, and a plain `romfs/` + `exefs/` folder, once the `<title ID>` level has been picked as the mods folder.
+- **Order.** Mods sort by folder name. The explicit `DM_ROMFS_OVERLAY` roots come first, then each mods folder in `DM_MODS_DIR` order and each mod in name order; the first root that has a path wins, and the base RomFS answers where none has it. Two mods that change the same file therefore have a defined winner (the first by name), not an error.
+- **What the overlay covers.** Loose files and files inside an unpacked RomFS only. A mod that replaces a file inside a packed archive (a `.pak`, a `.bundle`) needs that archive rebuilt; the overlay cannot see into it. The overlay applies to the `rom` mount, not to a DLC mount.
+- **Code mods.** The Windows front end applies them before the rewrite (`dm/tools/mod_apply.py`, the mods row's folder, `DM_MODS`): a replaced `<module>` (a full NSO) is staged for `patch_nso.py`, and `.ips` / `.pchtxt` patches are applied to the rewritten `<module>.patched.bin`; a build ID mismatch is refused, and a patch that touches a registered cut or a rewrite site is refused too, since it would undo a platform fix (`DM_MODS_ALLOW_CONFLICTS=1` applies it anyway and logs it). The build then runs the rewrite, `gen_embed.py`, the link and the IPA pack again, and the player reinstalls over the same bundle ID, which keeps saves and `Documents`. Extraction and shaders stay cached unless the mod changes them. A RomFS part of the same mod is copied into `<App>/mods/<title ID>/<mod>/` so it ships with the IPA, and `Documents/mods/<title ID>/` still wins at run time. A mod removed from the folder is undone on the next build: the pristine rewrite kept beside the patched module is restored.
+
